T1  - Beyond accuracy: Dual-layer opacity, knowledge gaps, and the trust-cliff dynamics of generative-AI markets
AU  - Nandagopal, Siddharth
JO  - Strategic Business Research
VL  - 1
IS  - 1
SP  - 100007
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025/12/01/
SN  - 3051-0643
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sbr.2025.100007
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S305106432500007X
KW  - Generative AI
KW  - Verifiable quality
KW  - Algorithmic opacity
KW  - Information asymmetry
KW  - Knowledge gap
KW  - Transparency governance
KW  - Risk-response cost
AB  - Digital markets depend on AI systems that both generate and surface content, with recommender pipelines increasingly powered by generative models alongside traditional ranking, making the visibility and use of quality signals central to trust and outcomes. Verifiable quality (Q̂) alone does not lower risk-response cost (R) when algorithmic opacity (O) and user knowledge-gaps (K) impede the perception and use of that quality, a pattern consistent with classic information-asymmetry logic and contemporary evidence on lifecycle opacity and human detection limits. This study develops a theory that explains and predicts when improvements in Q̂ fail to reduce R because O and K jointly attenuate the effect of quality, especially under dual-layer opacity spanning model pipelines and ranking algorithms. Using a hermeneutic, integrative literature-synthesis, I assemble and interpret evidence across economics, strategy, information systems, and AI governance to formalize the functional relation R = f(Q̂, O, K) and specify scope conditions and testable propositions. The core contribution is a conceptual model that identifies a “trust-cliff” region in which marginal gains in Q̂ have little observable effect on R until O and/or K are first reduced, reflecting “AI-generated lemons” dynamics. The paper translates this logic into an actionable program: deploy cryptographically-signed provenance and robust evaluations to raise Q̂, publish decision-usable disclosures to reduce O, and provide role-specific literacy and calibration to shrink K, aligned with emerging standards and regulatory duties. A forward agenda outlines field, experimental, and platform-audit designs to locate thresholds and boundary conditions across domains and modalities under current governance regimes.
ER  - 

