UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0031320326008733
KW  - Backdoor attack
KW  - Black-box model
KW  - General robust-feature
AB  - Backdoor attacks represent a significant security threat to deep neural networks. Existing attacks typically require strong assumptions — namely, the capacity to manipulate training data or to access victim models in a white-box manner — which fundamentally undermines their practicality in real-world settings. To address this limitation, we propose GeneralTrigger, a novel backdoor attack that functions under weak or even zero assumptions, demonstrating high transferability and attack success rate (ASR) comparable to traditional attacks when targeting black-box trained models. Specifically, we observe that the specific model relies on the overfitting model-specific robust-feature pattern for class recognition, whereas the more general model-agnostic robust-feature can be transferred across different models. Inspired by this observation, this work focuses on extracting the general robust-feature pattern and regarding it as trigger against victim models. To this end, we develop three key components for optimizing general robust-feature in GeneralTrigger: Sharpness-Aware Knowledge Distillation (SAKD), Model-Agnostic Joint Optimization (MAJO), and Sharpness-Aware Robust-Feature Optimization (SARFO). Extensive experiments demonstrate that GeneralTrigger successfully attacks 71 victim models across 4 datasets, achieving a maximum ASR of 98%–99%. Furthermore, even in the absence of training data, GeneralTrigger maintains an ASR of nearly 90%.
ER  - 

