AB  - ABSTRACT
This paper proposes a blockchain-anchored artificial intelligence (AI) ambassador framework for trustworthy digital consular services. The design integrates policy-aware retrieval-augmented generation, a permissioned blockchain for tamper-evident evidence commitments, privacy-preserving digital identity with selective disclosure, and governance safeguards for abstention, escalation, prompt and tool control, and red-team evaluation. An extended adoption model links perceived usefulness, ease of use, social influence, facilitating conditions, trust, and perceived risk to behavioral intention and observed use. Using a design science approach, the study performs an ex ante architectural assessment and defines a multi-method protocol for subsequent technical, expert, user, security, and adoption evaluation. The contribution is a traceable architecture and evaluation framework rather than evidence of operational deployment. It provides a foundation for accountable, privacy-aware, and auditable AI-enabled consular e-services while retaining human authority over legal, emergency, and other high-consequence decisions.
