AB  - Prompt learning has shown great potential for adapting Vision-Language Models (VLMs). Existing methods adopt a shared prompt across all inputs, failing to capture fine-grained semantic variations. Recent approaches incorporate input- or class-specific information, improving base-class performance. However, these approaches suffer from suboptimal generalization to novel categories. This highlights a fundamental challenge: designing prompting mechanisms that adapt to input-specific semantics while maintaining strong generalization. We propose Multi-modal Prompt Codebook Learning (MPCLe), a framework that introduces two shared, learnable prompt codebooks for vision and text. Instead of using fixed or instance-specific prompts, MPCLe dynamically selects a small subset of codebook tokens via attention scores to form input-adaptive prompts. To guide structured codebook learning, we introduce the Prompt Consistency and Diversity Loss (PCD Loss), which encourages similar prompt selections within the same class while promoting diversity across different classes. A lightweight Prompt-Aligned Adapter (PAA) further aligns multi-modal features into a unified space for classification. On the base-to-novel generalization benchmark averaged over 11 datasets, MPCLe achieves an average harmonic mean of 80.55%, outperforming the strong baseline PromptSRC (79.57%) by 0.98%, and obtains the highest accuracy of 67.02% on cross-dataset transfer and 60.86% on domain generalization. Our evaluation focuses on a CLIP model with a ViT-B/16 backbone.
