AB  - Due to their efficiency and capability to integrate distributed energy resources (DERs), DC microgrids (DCMGs) are becoming increasingly popular in renewable-energy systems, electric-vehicle charging, data centres, and industrial networks. Communication assisted control architectures, however, make them vulnerable to cyber–physical attacks like delay, packet loss, false data injection (FDI), denial-of-service, spoofing, and coordinated attacks. To date, reviews typically focus on one of the four themes of communication, cyber security, artificial intelligence (AI), and standardisation, and their respective interactions within DCMGs have not been sufficiently discussed. This paper is a PRISMA-based systematic review of 800 records identified, with 132 studies included. The review makes five major contributions. First, seven communication technologies are analysed using a uniform framework that correlates vulnerabilities in protocols to the quality of service (QoS) requirements of the DCM, such as: delay, packet loss, and synchronisation jitter. Second, the wired-wireless interoperability is discussed focusing on vulnerabilities arising from protocol translation. Third, single-point and coordinated multi-node FDI attacks are analysed separately and the implications for distributed detection and control are emphasized. Fourth, the resilience of federated learning is evaluated alongside Byzantine-robust aggregation, differential privacy, and secure model-update protocols, and their feasibility for resource-constrained edge controllers is evaluated. Finally, existing cybersecurity benchmarks are explored in conjunction with new frameworks for AI-governance to uncover unmet needs for AI-enabled DCMGs, which culminates in the suggested idea of AI resilience integrity levels (AILs). The review signalled a shift from traditional communication security to integrated AI-enabled cyber–physical resilience and pointed to the requirements for validation, governance and certification.
