AB  - Smart grid is evolving in different phases and artificial intelligence (AI) models are also a part of it. In the recent developments of smart grid systems, there are research areas which are left unaddressed specifically in privacy techniques, scalability, and more efficient data processing techniques. This proposed review highlights the research gaps in smart grid development across various integration phases. In the initial phase of smart grid development, challenges included scalability issues in 1) Machine-to-Machine (M2M) communication, 2) communication delays affecting voltage stabilization, and 3) high computational costs in implementing Genetic Algorithm (GA)-based grid state estimation, are covered. The proposed review is further extended to smart grid's expansion phase which focused on 5G and AI integration, while revealing difficulties in network reliability, latency, and electromagnetic interference-resistant protocols. In addition, the problems in implementing Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) and Augmented Reality (AR) for grid operations are considered. Grid interoperability through the use of digital technology has shown that there is a great need for the inclusion of edge controller technology and effective cyber-physical security mechanisms, which will be used to ensure that privacy protection requirements are met especially when using federated learning (FL) algorithms. This proposed article reveals how the integration of blockchain technology, AI, and Multi-Agent Deep Reinforcement Learning (MADRL) poses challenges associated with the optimization of real-time grid performance as well as securing and scaling up communication. The latest technologies include the use of UAVs, 6G, and meta-learning techniques that have proved effective but more work needs to be done.
