KW  - Accountability
KW  - Zero trust
KW  - Authentication
AB  - The proliferation of artificial intelligence, robotic process automation, and agentic systems in financial services has created a structural governance gap that existing cybersecurity identity frameworks were not designed to address: the human-machine identity continuum. Where traditional identity governance separated human users and machine accounts into discrete, separately managed domains, contemporary financial technology environments increasingly feature hybrid agents - systems that act with delegated human authority, make autonomous decisions within policy bounds, and generate audit trails that conflate human intent with machine execution. This paper identifies the human-machine identity blur as a distinct and critical cybersecurity governance challenge, and proposes the first integrated framework specifically designed to govern identity across the full continuum from human-controlled to fully autonomous agents. Using a structured comparative methodology, the paper evaluates six established identity governance frameworks - NIST SP 800–63, ISO/IEC 27,001:2022, FIDO2/WebAuthn, FATF Recommendation 16, the Zero Trust Architecture model, and PSD2/Open Banking standards - against six governance dimensions derived from the human-machine identity literature. The analysis demonstrates that no existing framework addresses more than two of six dimensions necessary for governing hybrid identity systems, and that all six frameworks fail to address accountability attribution and adaptive assurance calibration - the two dimensions most critical for managing identity risk in AI-augmented financial environments. The proposed framework introduces four governance components: identity classification taxonomy, contextual assurance calibration, accountability attribution mechanisms, and adaptive governance feedback. Scenario-based validation across four financial services identity failure archetypes - automated payment fraud, AI advisory overreach, deepfake social engineering, and insider-AI collusion - demonstrates that cross-layer identity governance misalignment explains systemic failures more completely than technical control analysis alone. The framework provides actionable guidance for cybersecurity practitioners, compliance officers, and regulators governing identity risk in AI-augmented financial services environments.
ER  - 

