KW  - Multimodal hallucination
AB  - Despite strong performance on multimodal understanding tasks, current Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) still exhibit premise blindness: when a question contains an assumption that contradicts the image, they often fabricate an answer based on that assumption instead of correcting it. Through paired diagnostic questions and controlled generation interventions, we show that models often have the visual evidence needed to reject a false premise, yet still follow that premise during generation and fabricate an answer. Our analysis further shows that questions with false and valid premises produce separable internal patterns; these patterns can shift early decoding preferences toward or away from correction, and interventions on the first generated token can recover or suppress corrective responses. Based on these findings, we propose two complementary methods, Verification-Mode Steering (VeMo) and Explicit Premise Verification (EPV). Specifically, VeMo selectively triggers corrective decoding based on internal representations, while EPV extracts implicit premises with an external LLM and verifies them through target LVLM queries before answering. Experiments across diverse LVLMs show that both methods substantially reduce premise blindness while largely preserving standard VQA behavior. These results suggest that mitigating premise blindness requires not only stronger visual recognition, but also better use of available visual evidence during generation.
