AB  - Recently, vision-language pre-trained models (VLPMs) have gained widespread attention due to their outstanding performance on cross-modal tasks. Multimodal alignment is a core technique in these models, involving the establishment of semantic correspondences between visual elements and linguistic units to achieve unified understanding and generation. As Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) emerge, alignment has evolved from simple feature mapping into a multidimensional optimization challenge encompassing architecture and policy encompassing both representational consistency and behavioral faithfulness. This paper provides a comprehensive review of over 80 core publications, innovatively proposing a dual-dimensional architecture: Representational Alignment and Behavioral Alignment. We systematically categorize state-of-the-art strategies into five key technical pillars: (1) Implicit Alignment, (2) Encoder-level Representational Alignment, (3) Fusion-stage Architectural Alignment, (4) Instruction and Preference-based Behavioral Alignment, and (5) Inference-time Dynamic Alignment. Finally, the paper summarizes critical challenges, including efficiency, social bias, and interpretability, and outlines a forward-looking research blueprint for unified multimodal systems.
