KW  - Human-in-the-loop
KW  - Hospital governance
KW  - Health informatics
AB  - The management of new medical technologies in hospitals has evolved from admission review to a longitudinal governance workflow covering application, expert evaluation, implementation, follow-up monitoring, completion review, and post-adoption oversight. Although existing digital platforms support rule encoding, node control, and status tracking, they remain limited in cross-system verification, semi-structured document handling, follow-up execution, and proactive risk intervention. Consequently, a persistent last-mile gap remains between anomaly detection and actual workflow closure. This commentary proposes a three-layer framework consisting of a rule center, an intelligent execution layer, and a human review layer, using OpenClaw as an illustrative example based on publicly available technical documentation. The framework is illustrated through a worked application-stage example and a future evaluation plan covering efficiency, quality, risk management, and management experience. The analysis suggests that the value of intelligent agents lies not in replacing existing platforms or human decision-making, but in functioning as an execution and connection layer outside the platform, extending anomaly detection to anomaly verification, classification, follow-up, and feedback. Under conditions of clear task boundaries, controllable permissions, and human review, intelligent agents may provide a potential pathway for improving workflow closure and refined governance in hospital management of new medical technologies.
