AB  - Automotive warranty records contain detailed evidence on field failures, repeated repairs, and repair actions, but heterogeneous fields and fragmented vehicle–part–fault relations make reliability-oriented synthesis difficult. We introduce a confidential industrial protocol for automotive warranty intelligence and evaluate an evidence-grounded framework over 16,948 anonymized after-sales records. The evaluated framework combines GraphRAG evidence packs from a 2500-record test-reference-enriched index, Dynamic ACE-Agent governance with an evidence workspace and frozen procedural memory, and retrieval-aware supervised fine-tuning. Eight system configurations were tested on a locked 150-item set covering single-record diagnosis, multi-record fault reasoning, and global reliability-evidence synthesis. The full system achieved the highest blind-judge integrated quality score (4.224/5 versus 3.272 for the Flat Hybrid-RAG baseline) and reduced the hard-error rate from 48.7% to 22.0%. In a stratified double-rated expert review of 45 items, it also achieved the highest weighted score (4.237), exceeding the strongest competing configuration by 0.199 points (95% confidence interval, 0.115–0.281; Holm-adjusted p=0.0131). Within this controlled protocol using a test-reference-enriched GraphRAG index, the integrated configuration produced the strongest observed traceable warranty responses.
