KW  - Certified removal
KW  - Deletion capacity
KW  - Influence functions
KW  - AI security
AB  - Machine unlearning enables trained models to comply with data deletion regulations by removing individual data points, but its interaction with adversarial robustness remains unexplored. We provide the first formal analysis of how Newton-step certified removal affects adversarial robustness, identifying unlearning as a new attack surface through which adversaries can compromise deployed models. We prove three results: (1) a per-deletion bound on adversarial margin sensitivity that is O(1/n); (2) a formula for robustness deletion capacity, the maximum adversarial deletions before the margin drops below a safety threshold, with an O(n) gap between adversarial and random orderings; and (3) a unidirectional noise-robustness tradeoff showing that certified removal noise always reduces margins, unlike the bidirectional noise-fairness tradeoff observed in prior work. Experiments on MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, and CIFAR-10 features confirm our theoretical predictions. For logistic regression, the minimum adversarial margin degrades monotonically under adversarial deletion orderings, and certified removal noise at ϵpriv=5.0 reduces adversarial accuracy by 66–69 percentage points on MNIST and Fashion-MNIST. Extending to a 2-layer MLP, adversarial deletions reduce both the margin and PGD accuracy by 9.7 percentage points at k=100, and certified removal noise collapses accuracy to random guessing. We propose concrete defense recommendations, including influence-based deletion screening and margin monitoring, to help practitioners secure unlearning-enabled systems.
