AB  - This chapter explores the intersection of ethics, culture, and technology, highlighting the urgent ethical concerns in the rapid evolution of artificial intelligence (AI) and its global impact, particularly in the health sector. Through historical reflections and modern philosophical critiques, it critiques the dominant Western liberalism principles, which often lack comprehensive metaphysical grounding. The chapter introduces the “Personalist Social Contract,” an ethical framework grounded in Thomistic-Aristotelian principles, proposing a global, multicultural ethics system. It emphasizes how AI platforms are increasingly central to global governance and how they shape ethical trajectories. The chapter unfolds examples such as Hippocratic AI and Anthropic's ethical structures, showcasing efforts to integrate human-centric governance through AI systems. Highlighting challenges like bias, rogue AI, and “alignment faking,” it proposes a more inclusive global AI framework rooted in collective human good and aligned with the ancient ideals of dignity and justice. It concludes by urging a shift from using technology as a mere tool of power to one of shared human well-being.
