DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cep.2026.110841
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0255270126001455
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Process intensification
KW  - Industrial automation
KW  - Smart manufacturing
AB  - The integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) with Process Intensification (PI) offers significant potential to enhance efficiency, sustainability, and operational flexibility in process industries. This work presents a structured critical review of AI applications in intensified processes, combining a systematic screening of recent literature with the analysis of representative industrial case studies. The methodology involves the classification of reported contributions according to application domain, AI technique and Technology Readiness Level (TRL), enabling the identification of gaps between academic developments and industrial deployment. Selected case studies, including AI-assisted optimization of dividing wall columns, predictive maintenance in petrochemical systems, real-time quality control in continuous pharmaceutical manufacturing, and adaptive optimization in biorefineries, demonstrate improvements in energy efficiency (10–30%), reduction of off-spec products (up to 70%), and enhanced process robustness under variable operating conditions. Despite these advances, most implementations remain at intermediate TRLs due to limitations in data infrastructure, model generalization, cybersecurity, and workforce capabilities. The analysis reveals that the lack of standardized validation frameworks and digital integration remains a key bottleneck for large-scale adoption. To overcome these challenges, this work proposes strategic directions focused on hybrid modeling approaches, standardized data architectures, and AI integration within process safety and control frameworks.
