UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167926026002440
KW  - Hardware Trojan detection
KW  - Machine learning
KW  - Design-for-security
KW  - Adversarial machine learning
KW  - FPGA security
KW  - Run time monitoring
AB  - The globalization of semiconductor design and manufacturing has increased the susceptibility of integrated circuits to Hardware Trojans (HTs), which are malicious modifications designed to evade detection through rare activation conditions, minimal structural footprints, and limited observability. As conventional detection techniques face increasing challenges related to scalability, low observability, and the growing complexity of modern circuits, machine learning (ML) has emerged as a promising data-driven approach for hardware Trojan detection. This paper presents a comprehensive survey of Hardware Trojan (HT) detection methodologies, with a primary focus on machine learning-based approaches, motivated by their rapid adoption and the growing need for a systematic understanding of feature representations, learning paradigms, robustness, and evaluation practices. The survey examines HT characteristics across multiple abstraction levels and reviews both classical and ML-based detection techniques. It further analyses the ML-based detection pipeline, including circuit representations, feature engineering, learning paradigms, graph-based learning, model architectures, feature selection strategies, and post-silicon detection approaches based on side-channel analysis, runtime monitoring, and online monitoring. Design-for-Security (DfS) techniques are also discussed as preventive mechanisms against Trojan insertion. Emerging challenges, including adversarial machine learning, explainability, dataset bias, FPGA deployment, and evaluation inconsistencies, are critically analysed from the perspectives of robustness and generalization. Unlike existing surveys, this work provides a unified analysis of feature engineering, graph-based representations, benchmarking, interpretability, adversarial robustness, and deployment considerations within a single ML-centric framework. Finally, the survey identifies key research gaps and highlights future research directions toward integrated, scalable, generalizable, and trustworthy hardware Trojan detection frameworks.
ER  - 

